    "lesson_index": 7,
    "lesson_title": "Cosine Modulation",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert multiplication by a cosine in time into the half-weighted sum of two shifted Fourier spectra centered at the modulation frequencies.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Cosine Modulation",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Cosine Modulation",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When you see $y(t)=x(t)\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$ and you already know $x(t)\\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$, read the cosine as a modulation cue: the spectrum will split into two shifted copies of $X(j\\omega)$, each with half weight.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(t)\\cos(\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Rewrite the cosine first. Euler's formula makes the two modulation directions visible at once, so the cosine becomes an average of one positive-frequency exponential and one negative-frequency exponential.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\cos(\\omega_0 t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(e^{j\\omega_0 t}+e^{-j\\omega_0 t}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now use linearity. The product becomes the sum of two terms, and the factor $1/2$ stays attached to both terms instead of disappearing.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the frequency-shift rule to each exponential factor. The $e^{j\\omega_0 t}$ term shifts the spectrum to $X(j(\\omega-\\omega_0))$, and the $e^{-j\\omega_0 t}$ term shifts it to $X(j(\\omega+\\omega_0))$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2}X(j(\\omega-\\omega_0))+\\frac{1}{2}X(j(\\omega+\\omega_0))",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So cosine modulation does not create a new spectrum shape. It produces two half-weighted copies of the original spectrum centered at $+\\omega_0$ and $-\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Cosine Modulation",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Writing a Cosine Product as Two Complex-Exponential Shifts",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $y(t)=x(t)\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$, rewrite the product as a half-weighted sum of two complex-exponential modulations of $x(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from $y(t)=x(t)\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$. The cue is the cosine factor, and the goal is to rewrite it as two equal-weight complex-exponential modulations of the same signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\cos(\\omega_0 t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(e^{j\\omega_0 t}+e^{-j\\omega_0 t}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute that identity into the product and distribute $x(t)$ across both terms. Keep the factor $1/2$ on each term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)e^{j\\omega_0 t}+\\frac{1}{2}x(t)e^{-j\\omega_0 t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the decomposition the next step will use: one cosine product becomes two half-weighted exponential modulations, one with $+\\omega_0$ and one with $-\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Cosine Modulation",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Computing the Two Half-Weighted Shifted Spectra",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)$ has CTFT $X(j\\omega)$, compute the CTFT of $x(t)\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from $x(t)\\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$ and the decomposition from the previous step. The cue is the two exponential factors: each one shifts the same spectrum instead of changing its shape.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)e^{j\\omega_0 t}+\\frac{1}{2}x(t)e^{-j\\omega_0 t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the frequency-shift rule to the positive exponential term first. Multiplying by $e^{j\\omega_0 t}$ moves the spectrum to the right by $\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)e^{j\\omega_0 t} &\\leftrightarrow X(j(\\omega-\\omega_0)) \\\\\nx(t)e^{-j\\omega_0 t} &\\leftrightarrow X(j(\\omega+\\omega_0))\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now attach the common $1/2$ weight to both shifted spectra and combine them. The negative exponential term shifts the spectrum to the left by $\\omega_0$, so both pieces stay balanced and separate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2}X(j(\\omega-\\omega_0))+\\frac{1}{2}X(j(\\omega+\\omega_0))",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So cosine modulation turns one product into two half-weighted spectral copies centered at $+\\omega_0$ and $-\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Cosine Modulation",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Applying Cosine Modulation to a Standard Transform Pair",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $e^{-t}u(t)$ has CTFT $1/(1+j\\omega)$, find the CTFT of $e^{-t}u(t)\\cos(3t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the known pair $e^{-t}u(t)\\leftrightarrow 1/(1+j\\omega)$. The cue is the cosine factor, so the earlier cosine-modulation rule applies directly and we do not re-derive the transform from scratch.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{-t}u(t),\\qquad X(j\\omega)=\\frac{1}{1+j\\omega}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the cosine-modulation formula with the numeric modulation frequency $\\omega_0=3$. That gives two shifted copies of the same standard spectrum, each with weight $1/2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2}X(j(\\omega-3))+\\frac{1}{2}X(j(\\omega+3))",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the standard pair into both shifted terms. The transform is just the original rational expression evaluated at $\\omega-3$ and $\\omega+3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\frac{1}{1+j(\\omega-3)}+\\frac{1}{1+j(\\omega+3)}\\right]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So multiplying $e^{-t}u(t)$ by $\\cos(3t)$ does not change the base spectrum shape. It produces the same spectrum centered at $+3$ and $-3$, with half weight on each copy.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Cosine Modulation",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Cosine Modulation as Two Shifted Spectral Copies",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $y(t)=e^{-t}u(t)\\cos(3t)$, interpret the spectrum as two half-height copies of the original spectrum centered at $\\omega=\\pm 3$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the base spectrum $X(j\\omega)=\\frac{1}{1+j\\omega}$ for $e^{-t}u(t)$. The cue is the shifted arguments in the cosine-modulation formula, so this step is about reading the final spectrum as a pair of copies of that same base shape.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\frac{1}{1+j(\\omega-3)}+\\frac{1}{1+j(\\omega+3)}\\right]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The term with $\\omega-3$ is the original spectrum shifted right by $3$, and the term with $\\omega+3$ is the same spectrum shifted left by $3$. Cosine modulation does not create a new shape; it places two copies of the base spectrum on the frequency axis.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Each shifted copy carries the factor $\\frac{1}{2}$, so each one has half the original height. That scaling is part of the cosine-modulation rule and applies to both sidebands equally.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the interpretation is two identical spectral copies centered at $\\omega=3$ and $\\omega=-3$, each with half amplitude. This is the reusable reading rule for cosine modulation: one base spectrum becomes two half-height sidebands at the modulation frequencies.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
